{
 "key": "pr-2420",
 "fields": {
  "summary": "fix(2362): nightly-red tracker self-heal — verified reopen with bounded retry + reopen-failed marker",
  "description": "Fixes #2362

## Root cause (run-log evidence)

The "monthly bot" is two bots, not one. The CLOSE leg is nightly.yml's auto-issue job (`github-actions[bot]`); the REOPEN leg never existed in this repo — it was a fleet-side monitor sweep (`vabhzw17eg2qu4m9-bit`; the #1953 timeline shows 18 reopens, every single one by that identity, normal latency 2–3.5 min). When that external watcher missed 2/2 nights (Nov 20: absent at 16.6 min → manual reopen, comment 7032755193; Nov 21: still closed at 20.8 min → manual reopen, comment 7058953000), nothing inside any bot run noticed. Close lands, reopen silently never does.

And the close leg itself fires on red nights. Evidence from run 44667132620 (2026-11-20; identically 44860623417 on 2026-11-21):

- jobs API: `PTY/CLI integration (nightly-only suites)` FAILED at 03:33:10Z, `Build ios (release)` FAILED at 03:25:37Z;
- auto-issue job log (job 136131318071), line 67: `decision: closed #1953 — nightly green again` at 06:40:02Z;
- run conclusion: `failure` at 06:40:05Z — 3 seconds AFTER the close.

Mechanism: auto-issue runs under job-level `if: always() && !cancelled()`, and step-level `success()` (close step) / `failure()` (filing step) see only the job's OWN steps — never the `needs` failures. So the close step fires on EVERY scheduled night with an open tracker, red or green, and the red-night filing path can never fire. Corroborated: exactly one nightly-red issue ever existed (#1953) — authored by the fleet, not by the filer.

## Delivered

- `scripts/nightly_red_tracker.sh` — self-heal leg invoked by nightly.yml right after the close step, inside the same run:
  - green night → close stands, zero mutations;
  - red night (any job `conclusion == "failure"` in this run — the same jobs-API query the filer step uses) → tracker re-opened back-to-back, OPEN state verified with ONE bounded retry (reopen → re-check after N seconds → retry once) = **AC1**;
  - retry exhaustion → `reopen-failed` label + comment + nonzero exit = **AC2** (loud, no 15-min SLA polling timer).
- nightly.yml close leg unchanged (same comment/close/why-not decisions = **AC3**); it only exports `closed_number` so the self-heal pairs with THIS run's close. Two steps added after it: pinned checkout (this job previously had none — same pattern as the ai-teammate gate) and the schedule-only self-heal step.
- `scripts/nightly_red_tracker_selftest.sh` — fixture selftest (stubbed gh, real jq doing the filtering, no network; #1100 pattern) — 18 checks across AC1–AC5, including the exact observed Nov-20 job shape (PTY/CLI + ios failed, needs-green legs, in-flight filer). Wired into ci.yml Static gates next to the flake-watcher selftest.
- CHANGELOG.md Unreleased entry.

Gates run locally: `bash -n` on both scripts; selftest all green (18/18); both workflows YAML-validated (5 jobs nightly / 55 jobs ci). shellcheck is not installed on this host — not run.

## Remaining / deliberately out of scope

- The filer's red-night path (`Create or update the nightly-red issue`, `if: failure()`) still cannot fire on a scheduled run — same step-level semantics, but fixing it would change the filer, which the AC3 pin does not cover; named here, not touched. With the self-heal leg, the tracker ends every red night OPEN again, which is also the precondition for the dedup-comment path to work once it gets fixed.
- The fleet-side monitor sweep can remain as a redundant safety net; it is no longer load-bearing for the reopen."
 }
}

